plan: Keep the completed W chow, discard one of the isolated tiles. Between T3 and T7, which is less likely to be needed by opponents? Looking at opponents' melds: previous player has J1 pung, so they might be collecting honors. Next player has no melds. Across player has B7,B8,B9 chow. So nobody seems to be collecting T suit aggressively. T3 and T7 are both relatively safe.\n\nConsidering the \"All Chows\" pattern (worth 2 points), we already have three chows exposed, and if we get a fourth chow (W chow) and a pair, we would qualify for All Chows. That's a good basic pattern. Also, we might get \"Pure Double Chow\" if we have two identical chows? But we don't. We might get \"Mixed Double Chow\"? Our chows are in different suits. So likely just All Chows and maybe some other small patterns.\n\nWe also have no honors, so \"No Honors\" (1 point) might apply. Also, if we self-draw, \"Self Drawn\" (1 point). But we need at least 8 points to win. So we need more patterns. Currently, if we win with All Chows (2), No Honors (1), Self Drawn (1), that's only 4 points. We need more. Possibly \"All Simples\" (2 points) if our hand has no terminals or honors. Check our tiles: W4,W5,W6 are simples (2-8). T3 and T7 are simples. Our melds: T3,T4,T5 (simples), B4,B5,B6 (simples), T4,T5,T6 (simples). So yes, all simples, that's 2 points. Total so far: All Chows (2) + No Honors (1) + All Simples (2) = 5 points. Still need 3 more. Could get \"Pure Double Chow\" if we have two identical chows? We don't. \"Short Straight\"? Possibly if we have two chows in same suit with successive numbers: we have T3,T4,T5 and T4,T5,T6 which are successive? Actually, they are overlapping but not successive as chows; they share numbers. Short Straight requires two chows like 234 and 567. Ours are 345 and 456, which are consecutive but overlapping; not sure if that counts. Usually Short Straight is for two chows that form a sequence like 234567, i.e., 234 and 567. Ours are 345 and 456, which are not that