ng else that pairs with something? For example, if we draw W4, we could break the W chow: use two W4 as pair, and then we have W5,W6 and T7, which need a W4 or W7 to form a chow? That would be a different winning shape, but less likely. So our primary wait is for T7. That's a single wait.\n\nNow, check if we can get enough points: All Chows (2), All Simples (2), No Honors (1), Single Wait (1), Self Drawn (1) = 7 points. Still short of 8. Need one more point. Could be from \"Concealed Hand\"? But we have exposed melds, so not. \"Tile Hog\"? Unlikely. \"Pung of Terminals or Honors\"? None. \"Mixed Double Chow\"? We have two chows in different suits with same numbers? Our chows are T345, B456, T456. The T345 and T456 are not identical numbers. B456 and T456 are same numbers? B456 and T456 have the same numbers (4,5,6) but different suits. That is a Mixed Double Chow! Actually, Mixed Double Chow is two chows in different suits with the same numbers. We have [B4,B5,B6] and [T4,T5,T6] \u2014 that's exactly a Mixed Double Chow, worth 1 point. And it can be counted once per set. So we have one Mixed Double Chow already. That gives 1 point. So total becomes 8 points: All Chows (2), All Simples (2), No Honors (1), Single Wait (1), Self Drawn (1), Mixed Double Chow (1) = 8 points. Perfect. If we don't self-draw but win by discard, we lose Self Drawn but still have 7, need another. But we might get \"Last Tile\" or something else. But at least we have a path to 8 points.\n\nThus, discarding T3 seems fine.\n\nBut also consider if we discard T7, we wait for T3. Similar calculation. But T3 has fewer copies (3 vs 4), so slightly lower probability. Also, T3 might be more dangerous because it's closer to our own melds, but opponents may not need it.\n\nI'll choose to discard T3.\n\nNow, check valid actions: Play W6; Play T3; Play T7; Play W5; Play W4. So we can discard any of these. I'll choose Play T3.\n\nReason: To keep the ready W4-W5-W6 chow and wait for T7 to complete the pair, aiming for a winning hand with All Chows, All Simples, No Honors, Mixed Double Chow, and Single Wait, which meets the 8-point requirement.\n\nAnswer: Play T3"}
