o chows including W2,W3,W4 and B7,B8,B9. Next player has no melds.  \n\nYour goal is to discard a tile that is relatively safe (not likely to give others a pung/kong/win) while keeping your hand flexible. From the unshown tiles count:  \n- W4: 3 left (you have 1, total 4, none seen in discards? Not in history except maybe in melds \u2014 across player has W2,W3,W4 chow, so one W4 is used, leaving 3 in wall).  \n- W5: 4 left (you have 1).  \n- W6: 4 left (you have 1).  \n- T7: 4 left (you have 1).  \n- B5: 3 left (you have 1, one used in your meld [B4,B5,B6], so total 4, 1 in hand, 1 in meld, 2 left? Actually unshown says B5:3, meaning 3 left in wall plus the one in your hand? The unshown count includes tiles not yet drawn, so B5 total 4 copies, you have 1 in hand and 1 in meld, so 2 left in wall? Wait, unshown tiles count is supposed to be remaining in wall + other players' hands? Usually it's total remaining unseen. Given melds: your meld used B5, across player none, previous none, next none. So B5 used: 1 (your meld). Discards: B5 not discarded? In history, you played B3, B1, etc. No B5 discard. So B5 unseen total = 4 total - 1 (your meld) - 1 (your hand) = 2 left in wall. But unshown says B5:3, meaning 3 left unseen (including your hand? Possibly counts your hand as unseen to others). Actually unshown list likely counts tiles not visible to you, so your hand tiles are included. So B5:3 means there are 3 B5 not visible to you (including in other players' hands and wall). Since you have 1 B5 in hand, that means wall+others have 2 B5.  \n\nSafety:  \n- W4: Across player has W2,W3,W4 chow, so they might need W1 or W5 for related chows, but W4 itself is less likely to be needed for pung since they have one already.  \n- W5: Many left, safe.  \n- W6: Many left, safe.  \n- T7: Many left, safe.  \n- B5: Relatively safe, but could be needed for chow by others (B3,B4,B5 or B5,B6,B7).  \n\nYour hand structure: You have three chows exposed, all are simple tiles (no terminals/honors). You might aim for \"All Chows\" (2 points) if you get one more chow and a pair. Also \"All Simples\" (2 points) possible. Also maybe \"Mixed Triple Chow\"? Your exposed chows: [T3,T4,T5], [B4,B5,B6], [T4,T5,T6] \u2014 two are in T suit with overlapping numbers, not identical. So not triple chow.  \n\nIf you discard a tile from the W4,W5,W6 chow, you break a ready chow. If you keep it, you have a concealed chow, but you still need a pair. The pair could be T7 or B5 if you draw another one. Alternatively, you could discard T7 or B5 and try to draw a pair for the remaining tile.  \n\nGiven you have 5 tiles, you need 4 tiles to complete a standard hand (one more meld + pair). Actually, standard hand: 14 tiles total. You have 5 in hand + 9 in melds (3 chows of 3 tiles each) = 14 total. So you actually have a complete hand? Wait, check: 3 melds = 9 tiles, hand = 5 tiles, total 14. That is a complete set of tiles. But you need a winning hand structure: either 4 melds + 1 pair, or seven pairs, or special. Currently you have 3 melds + 5 hand tiles. The 5 hand tiles could be arranged as one chow (W4,W5,W6) + a pair (say T7,T7) + a single (B5) \u2014 but you only have one T7. Or as a pair (B5,B5) + a chow (W4,W5,W6) + single T7 \u2014 but you only one B5. So not complete. To win, you need to either:  \n- Form a pair and a chow from your 5 tiles (e.g., draw a T7 to pair with your T7, and keep W4,W5,W6 as chow, then you have 4 chows + pair).  \n- Or draw a tile that completes a chow with two of your singles (e.g., draw B4 to go with B5,B6? But B6 not in hand, you have B5 only. Or draw T6 to go with T7,T8? Not in hand).  \n\nSo you are not ready. You need to discard one tile to get to 4 tiles in hand (since total tiles must remain 14). After discard, you'll have 4 hand tiles + 3 melds = 13 tiles, waiting for 14th to win.  \n\nThus, you should discard the least useful tile. Which tile is least useful for forming a meld/pair?  \n- W4, W5, W6 together form a chow, so discarding any breaks it. But if you keep them, you have a ready chow, but you still need a pair from the other two tiles (T7 and B5). So you need duplicates of T7 or B5.  \n- Alternatively, you could break the W chow and try to pair one of W4/W5/W6 with a duplicate, and make a chow from T7 with other tiles (need T5,T6 or T8,T9). But T5,T6 are mostly used (your melds have T4,T5,T6 and T3,T4,T5), so T5 and T6 are less available.  \n\nGiven the unshown counts:  \nT7: 4 left \u2014 good chance to draw pair.  \nB5: 3 left \u2014 good chance to draw pair.  \nW4: 3 left \u2014 possible to draw pair but less likely since you'd need to break chow.  \n\nSo best to keep T7 and B5 as potential pair candidates, and keep W4,W5,W6 as a chow. Then you need one of T7 or B5 to pair. So discard one of W4,W5,W6? But if you discard one, you lose the chow and then you have 4 singles: say discard W4, left W5,W6,T7,B5 \u2014 no meld, need two melds+pair. That's worse. So you must keep the W chow.  \n\nThus, you cannot discard W4, W5, or W6 because they form your only complete meld in hand. You must discard either T7 or B5.  \n\nBetween T7 and B5:  \n- T7 is a terminal in the sense it's 7 of Bamboo, but not terminal 1 or 9, so it's a simple tile.  \n- B5 is a simple tile.  \nWhich is more dangerous? Looking at opponents' melds:  \n- Across player has B7,B8,B9 chow, so might need B6 for chow, B5 less likely.  \n- Previous player has J1 pung, no B suits shown.  \n- Next player no melds.  \nDiscard history: You have played T3, B3, B1, etc. Others have played T9, B7, etc.  \nB5 has not been discarded. T7 has not been discarded.  \n\nBoth seem safe. However, B5 is a middle tile often used in chows. T7 is edge tile (7) useful for chows 5,6,7 or 7,8,9. Given across player has B7,B8,B9, they might want T7? No, different suit. They might want W7? Not relevant.  \n\nGiven your own melds include T4,T5,T6 and T3,T4,T5, T7 could combine with T5,T6 to form chow? But T5,T6 are used in your melds, so less available. Still, T7 might be slightly more dangerous because it's a 7, and someone might have T6,T7 waiting for T8 or T5,T6 waiting for T7. But since many T7 are left (4), risk is low.  \n\nI think discarding B5 is slightly better because it's a 5, central, but your own meld has B4,B5,B6, so you've already used one B5, reducing others' chance to have B5-based chows.  \n\nBut also consider scoring: If you win with pair of B5, you get no extra pattern except maybe \"All Chows\" and \"All Simples\". If you win with pair of T7, same.  \n\nSo choose discard that keeps flexibility: If you discard B5, you keep T7 as pair candidate and W chow. Then you need to draw another T7. If you discard T7, you need to draw another B5. Both similar.  \n\nCheck unshown: T7: 4 left, B5: 3 left, so T7 has more copies left, slightly better chance to draw pair. So discard B5 to keep T7 as pair candidate.  \n\nAlso, B5 is more likely to be used by others for chow (since B4 and B6 are less available? Unshown B4:1 left, B6:2 left, so B4 is rare, B6 somewhat rare). Actually, B4 only 1 left, so B4,B5,B6 chow is unlikely for others. B5,B6,B7 chow: B6 2 left, B7 2 left, possible. Still, risk moderate.  \n\nI'll choose discard B5.  \n\nReason: Keeping the concealed chow of W4,W5,W6 maintains a ready meld, and keeping T7 gives a better chance to form a pair since there are 4 T7 left unseen. Discarding B5 is relatively safe and allows focusing on drawing T7 or possibly converting to other patterns.  \n\nAnswer: Play B5"}
